   "kind": "structure",
   "title": "The irregular-tape holdout halts only if a chaotic word eats the whole run",
   "text": "Next to its run of 1s this machine keeps a short word W that changes like a small chaotic engine rather than a counter. Lean proves the machine halts exactly when W eats the whole run of 1s. An exact run of 3&middot;10<sup>9</sup> visits (8.6&middot;10<sup>18</sup> steps) never saw W eat more than 18 cells in one visit. A proof would need a bound on one visit's bite that holds for every word the engine can produce, and none is in sight.",
   "rests": "lean",
   "machines": [
    "1RB1RA_1LC1RE_---0LD_1LE1LD_0RA0LF_1LF0LB"
   ],
   "tag": "irregular-tape holdout"
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-11-20",
   "prs": [
    326,
    320,
    308
   ],
   "kind": "bound",
   "title": "The collapse pair: an exact cycle law, and no halt to 6.1&middot;10<sup>135</sup> steps",
   "text": "These two machines are the same nested ternary counter: the tape is a list of segments of 111-blocks drained by 3<sup>j</sup> per event, and segments run out one after another (collapses). After each collapse the counter settles into a new regime of the same kind. All 71 cycles observed follow one exact law (the oldest segment loses 2, the j-th loses 3<sup>j</sup>, the steps grow ninefold), and the next two collapses were run ahead of time without a halt. A Lean proof needs a new invariant first, because sweeps at the far end keep adding rules.",
   "rests": "computation",
   "machines": [
    "1RB0LB_1RC0LA_1LB0RD_1RE1RD_1RB1RF_1LA---",
    "1RB0LC_1LA0RD_1RA0LA_1RE1RD_1RA1RF_1LC---"
   ],
   "tag": "collapse pair"
  },
